1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,625
These secondary structure elements sit in one chain, and they have positions in space relative to one another that are particular to that protein.

2
00:00:08,625 --> 00:00:14,275
That arrangement is the tertiary structure, and it covers a single polypeptide chain.

3
00:00:14,275 --> 00:00:23,125
What holds it together is the hydrophobic effect, together with non-covalent interactions between atoms of both the backbone and the side chains.

4
00:00:23,725 --> 00:00:27,725
There are four kinds of non-covalent interaction worth knowing.

5
00:00:27,725 --> 00:00:31,300
Van der Waals interactions have two parts.

6
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:38,350
The attraction, which reaches further, comes from quantum-mechanical fluctuations in the dipole moments of the atoms.

7
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:47,875
The repulsion, which is very short range, comes from electron clouds overlapping, from the nuclei repelling each other, and from the Pauli exclusion principle.

8
00:00:47,875 --> 00:00:51,500
Electrostatic interactions give salt bridges.

9
00:00:51,500 --> 00:01:00,800
A salt bridge forms between two charged residues — lysine or arginine on the positive side, aspartic or glutamic acid on the negative.

10
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:11,950
Hydrogen bonds are interactions between dipoles, which arise because charge is distributed unevenly between the nitrogen and oxygen of the backbone and of the polar side chains.

11
00:01:11,950 --> 00:01:18,925
And disulphide bonds are strong — nearly as strong as covalent bonds — formed between two sulphur atoms.

12
00:01:18,925 --> 00:01:23,375
Only cysteine can do this; the sulphur in methionine cannot.

13
00:01:23,975 --> 00:01:30,625
Water molecules form hydrogen bonds with each other, and that is what makes water such an unusual liquid.

14
00:01:30,625 --> 00:01:35,775
One consequence matters a great deal for proteins: the hydrophobic effect.

15
00:01:35,775 --> 00:01:44,800
A carbon atom cannot form a hydrogen bond, so when one arrives the water molecules rearrange themselves to surround and isolate the intruder.

16
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:54,350
If there are two such atoms, the rearrangement ends up excluding water from the space between them, and the two are sometimes said to form a hydrophobic bond.

17
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:59,125
The hydrophobic effect is the driving force behind protein folding.

18
00:01:59,725 --> 00:02:07,800
Proteins also form complexes — two chains, three chains, and in some experimentally determined structures hundreds of them.

19
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:15,650
How the tertiary structures of the individual chains are arranged relative to each other is the quaternary structure, the highest level.

20
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:29,475
It is held together by the same two things: the hydrophobic effect, because trapping water between hydrophobic patches of two surfaces costs energy, and non-covalent interactions between atoms belonging to different chains.

21
00:02:30,075 --> 00:02:36,050
And this is the classical summary slide: all four levels of protein structure in one picture.

22
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:43,950
Experimentally determined structures live in the Protein Data Bank, and predicted ones in the AlphaFold database.

23
00:02:43,950 --> 00:02:47,100
Both came up in the first lecture of the course.

24
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:50,100
What is worth looking at here is the growth.

25
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:55,350
The yellow bars are the structures deposited each year, against the running total.

26
00:02:55,350 --> 00:03:05,250
The plot on the right shows exactly the same data on a logarithmic scale — useful whenever you want two quantities on one plot that differ by orders of magnitude.

27
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:14,825
Structures in the Protein Data Bank come from three methods: X-ray crystallography, nuclear magnetic resonance, and electron microscopy.

28
00:03:14,825 --> 00:03:18,800
The interesting thing is the trend over the last twenty years.

29
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:30,825
Electron microscopy has become steadily more popular, and because of it the database now holds entire macromolecular assemblies — ribosomes, viral capsids — at atomic resolution.

30
00:03:31,425 --> 00:03:37,825
The second part of the lecture is about comparing structures, which is something you will need to do constantly.

31
00:03:37,825 --> 00:03:43,600
Tertiary and quaternary structures are treated separately, though some measures work for both.

32
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:51,300
Before the measures themselves, one thing that courses often skip: how to move an object around in three-dimensional space.

33
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:54,150
Most comparison metrics need it.

34
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:57,375
It is called a rigid-body transformation.

35
00:03:57,375 --> 00:03:59,400
Then three measures.

36
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:05,625
Root-mean-square deviation and the TM-score both depend on moving one structure onto the other.

37
00:04:05,625 --> 00:04:08,300
The GDT score does not.

38
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:16,700
A rigid-body transformation moves an object through space without changing the positions of its parts relative to one another.

39
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:24,750
For a protein, that means moving the whole tertiary structure while leaving the secondary structure elements exactly where they are within it.

40
00:04:24,750 --> 00:04:33,100
Mathematically it is a function: give it the coordinates of every atom in the old position, and it returns their coordinates in the new one.

41
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:35,700
It has two parts.

42
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:42,750
First a translation — the centre of mass moves along some direction, described by a translation vector.

43
00:04:42,750 --> 00:04:49,575
Then a rotation about that centre in its new position, described by a three-by-three rotation matrix.

44
00:04:49,575 --> 00:04:52,950
The compact form of that function is on the slide.

45
00:04:53,550 --> 00:05:01,500
Those two things — the translation vector and the rotation matrix — are what most structural superposition programs hand back to you.

46
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:05,975
Here is a real example: the output of TM-align.

47
00:05:05,975 --> 00:05:09,825
The matrix and the vector are right there in the text it prints.

48
00:05:10,425 --> 00:05:16,500
The same transformation written out algebraically, in a form you could type straight into a program.

49
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:20,725
There is a more compact way to describe the rotation.

50
00:05:20,725 --> 00:05:26,025
Instead of the nine numbers of a rotation matrix, three will do: the Euler angles.

51
00:05:26,025 --> 00:05:29,250
Their relation to the matrix is on the slide.

52
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:35,900
This is the most widely used way of comparing two structures: the root-mean- square deviation.

53
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:44,775
If you know which atom in protein A corresponds to which atom in protein B, you can measure the distance between each pair and take the root mean square.

54
00:05:44,775 --> 00:05:48,025
But there is a catch, and the two pictures show it.

55
00:05:48,025 --> 00:05:56,525
Those distances depend on where the proteins happen to sit in space — enormous when the two are far apart, small when they are superimposed.

56
00:05:56,525 --> 00:06:00,825
So you never calculate RMSD on the coordinates as they come.

57
00:06:00,825 --> 00:06:08,850
You first transform one structure onto the other, and the transformation you want is the one that makes the RMSD as small as it can be.

58
00:06:08,850 --> 00:06:14,700
If two structures are identical, the optimal superposition gives an RMSD of zero.

59
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:18,975
Two things worth remembering about structural alignment.

60
00:06:18,975 --> 00:06:22,125
Structure is more conserved than sequence.

61
00:06:22,125 --> 00:06:28,775
And there is no such thing as structural homology — homology is a statement about ancestry, not about shape.

62
00:06:29,375 --> 00:06:35,125
One point that gets overlooked: the root-mean-square deviation is not the average distance.

63
00:06:35,125 --> 00:06:46,575
Squaring first and taking the root afterwards gives more weight to the large deviations, so a single badly placed region moves the RMSD far more than its share of the atoms would suggest.

64
00:06:47,175 --> 00:06:52,325
The second measure is the TM-score, which comes out of the program TM-align.

65
00:06:52,325 --> 00:06:58,825
Variants of it — MM-align and I-align — are used for quaternary structures and for interface fragments.

66
00:06:58,825 --> 00:07:07,600
In outline, TM-align first builds a residue-to-residue alignment from structural similarity, using repeated rounds of dynamic programming.

67
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:15,975
It then measures the distances between the aligned atoms in that superposition and normalises them into a score between zero and one.

68
00:07:15,975 --> 00:07:21,350
The formula is on the slide, and the original paper is from two thousand and five.

69
00:07:21,950 --> 00:07:26,175
The third measure does not depend on superimposing anything.

70
00:07:26,175 --> 00:07:28,925
It is the global distance test.

71
00:07:28,925 --> 00:07:35,050
It works from the fraction of distances that are conserved in the model compared with the reference structure.

72
00:07:35,050 --> 00:07:39,300
Two distances are drawn here, one conserved and one not.

73
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:43,750
Whether a distance counts as conserved is a judgement, not a fact.

74
00:07:43,750 --> 00:07:49,975
The example on the slide is conserved at a threshold of two ångström, and would not be at one.

75
00:07:49,975 --> 00:07:54,425
That is why these scores are computed at several thresholds and averaged.

76
00:07:54,425 --> 00:08:01,775
There is a local version too, the local distance difference test, which works the same way over a different set of atoms.

